## What it is

`[WebServer]` = Playwright forwarding the Next.js dev server's own stderr into your test console (`playwright.config.ts:114` sets `stdout: 'pipe'` / `stderr: 'pipe'`).

`Error: The destination stream closed early.` is a Next.js internal error: it tried to write to an HTTP response stream whose socket the client had **already closed**. The `digest` + `at ignore-listed frames` (collapsed stack) is Next flagging it as a framework-internal, non-actionable error — it does **not** fail any test and Playwright doesn't treat it as one.

## Why it fires constantly in the E2E run

Almost certainly your SSE endpoint, `src/app/api/events/route.ts`. Every page that renders `<AutoRefresh>` opens an `EventSource` per channel (`useAutoRefresh.ts:37`). Playwright navigates fast and runs specs in parallel, so those connections are torn down all the time, and the route has a race:

```ts
const listener = () => {
    controller.enqueue(encoder.encode('event: update\ndata: {}\n\n')); // ← throws if socket already gone
};
emitter.on(channel, listener);
request.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => { emitter.off(...); controller.close(); });
```

If `emitter` emits in the window between the browser dropping the connection and the `abort` handler running, `enqueue` lands on a dead stream → "closed early". Normal streaming-SSR pages aborted mid-navigation produce the same message, but the SSE path is what makes it *repeat a lot*.

## It's harmless

Same meaning in production: "client hung up before we finished sending." Nothing to fix for correctness.

## If you want to silence it

Harden the SSE route so it never writes after close:

```ts
start(controller) {
    let closed = false;
    const listener = () => {
        if (closed || request.signal.aborted) return;
        try {
            controller.enqueue(encoder.encode('event: update\ndata: {}\n\n'));
        } catch {
            // stream already torn down; ignore
        }
    };
    emitter.on(channel, listener);
    request.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
        closed = true;
        emitter.off(channel, listener);
        controller.close();
    });
},
```

That removes the SSE-origin instances. The occasional one from aborted page streaming is framework-level — live with it, or set `webServer.stdout: 'ignore'` in the Playwright config to drop dev-server chatter entirely (you'd also lose genuine server logs during E2E).

Want me to apply the SSE hardening? It's a real (if tiny) robustness improvement, not just noise suppression.

